EXT. CITY HALL PLAZA - CLIMATE PROTEST - DAY

SUPER: "THURSDAY, 2:14 PM"

A river of banners and drums and kids on shoulders pours across the plaza, whistles shrieking and a brass band somewhere playing something almost like a hymn, and at the edge of the crowd under a dead plane tree four people in orange vests drift together, not looking at one another, while a police helicopter chops overhead and a woman on a megaphone leads a chant that rolls back and forth like surf.

INSERT - A CITY MAP on a phone, the bank across the plaza circled in red.

SUPER: "ARCHIVE VAULT, FLOOR B2"

The four close ranks. MIRA ADEYEMI, 30s, sunglasses pushed up, holds a placard that says THERE IS NO PLANET B. Beside her, TOBIAS WREN, 50s, the team's old safe man, carries a cardboard sun on a stick.

MIRA
(low, smiling for the crowd)
Everybody breathe. We hold the crowd until the alarm trips, the guards come out, and then we walk in through the loading dock like we're delivering pamphlets. Forty seconds. That's the whole plan.

TOBIAS
(waving the cardboard sun)
You said that about the last one.

MIRA
(beat)
The last one had a dog.

JUNO PARK, 20s, in a hand-knit hat, scrolls through a message thread on her phone, a chant in her ears.

JUNO
(typing)
Dock crew says the guards changed shift early. Two minutes ago. They won't be where we mapped them.

MIRA
(lowering her voice)
Which means we move now, not later. Tobias, the code?

TOBIAS
(patting his coat)
Right here in my pocket, same as it's been for eleven years, and if you look at it, Mira, I swear on my mother's pension I will fall apart.

JUNO
(quietly, not looking up)
The sun's wobbling.

TOBIAS
(adjusting the stick)
It's a very old sun.

The brass band swells. A drum thunders. The crowd surges left, a wall of orange and green flags.

RAFA DELACROIX, 40s, ear-piece in, drifts into the circle from the back, face grey, shoulder-checking the crowd.

RAFA
(under his breath)
We have a problem. One of the vests across the plaza has a radio and a plain jacket underneath. They're not guards. They're federal.

MIRA
(a long beat, lips barely moving)
Then they are here for someone else.

RAFA
(shaking his head)
Mira. They are here for us.

TOBIAS
(lowering the cardboard sun slowly)
Well. That is a very short plan.

JUNO
(reading, voice flat)
Message from the dock. It says: WHERE ARE YOU. IT'S NOT SAFE. LEAVE NOW.

MIRA
(closing her eyes, then opening them, quiet)
Everybody walk. Don't run. Join the march, lose the vests, and meet at the van. Nobody looks back.

RAFA
(softly)
And the vault?

MIRA
(gently, to the crowd, the sky, to herself)
The vault has waited a hundred years. It can wait another one.

The chant swells. Rain begins, thin and sudden. The four melt into the crowd one by one.

EXT. CITY HALL PLAZA - LATER

Banners droop. The brass band packs its cases. A single cardboard sun lies face down in a puddle, its stick snapped in half.
